# .release-removals — files this release deliberately removes from the published tree.
#
# One path or path-prefix per line; `#` comments allowed. A removal is not forbidden,
# but it must be intended, and this file is the record that it was. Where something
# moved rather than went away, the entry says where to, so a reader can follow it.
#
# A comment-only or blank file declares nothing.

# ── 0.10.0 removes nothing ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Measured against the 0.9.0 tree rather than assumed: every one of the 739 files
# published at 0.9.0 is still here, and every document that was in the reading
# order still is. One document was ADDED to it — the service-worker kill-switch
# runbook, which the 0.9.0 release notes referred you to without shipping.

# ── STATUS.md moved up one directory ──────────────────────────────────────────
# 2026-08-24. The rolling status document was at `docs/status/STATUS.md`. Everything
# else in `docs/status/` is dated, session-scoped material that is not part of the
# published documentation set, so the one durable file in there was the exception —
# and an exception inside a directory whose whole point is that it is ephemeral is a
# shape that eventually goes wrong.
#
# It is now **`docs/STATUS.md`**, which is also where the other repos in this
# ecosystem keep theirs. Nothing is withdrawn from a reader: the same document ships,
# one directory up.
docs/status/STATUS.md
